               Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free:
                  Two Registered Tests of an Audit Rule

                                              Ömer Demirel

                                        Draft 9, November 2026


                                                  Abstract
          A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider
      can return at once under a new identity. In a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery,
      costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, we show that for the
      uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement the ban costs nothing,
      because replacement restores eligibility and the payoff-relevant future states, given honest rivals
      whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a
      new identity while the budget allows and then keeps targeting the newest identities. A registered
      test compares it with a verified-history comparator, C∗ , at matched budget and sanctions on six
      markets drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash. Four findings. First, pooled over the six
      markets, R3 and C∗ are “equivalent, direction R3 lower” within a pre-set margin of ±0.05 (C∗
      minus R3 under D2+H3 attacks: 0.021); an exploratory t interval over the six market differences,
      [−0.013, 0.054], does not establish the ordering beyond these markets, and the second attack
      family repeated the first family’s winner in 10 of 12 cells. Second, newest-first targeting does
      almost all the work: once it is in place, R3’s priority window changes the attacker’s gain by
      only −0.001. Third, at the headline market the targeting also falls on an honest newcomer: R3
      audits 0.917 of a replacement identity’s jobs in its second ten rounds, C∗ 0.161. Fourth, under
      D2+H3, R3 lowers the attacker’s gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the
      registered replication passes on 3 of six; at the headline market, welfare attacks lower all three
      rules’ gross efficiency to about 0.840. The claims hold for the prescribed searches and frozen
      references, the evaluated markets, fixed parameters, the searched families, and honest opponents
      that never replace their identities, so honest churn is untested; C∗ was selected in development
      on gross efficiency in effect.


1    Introduction
A buyer of computation pays for work whose quality it cannot see. It can check a few jobs at a cost
and sanction a provider whose check fails. Exact checks of computation now exist, but they are
expensive and run after the response is served. FlashProve, for example, proves that a committed
integer model produced a given token under greedy decoding; the provider generates proofs after the
response is served, at about 70 to 95 times the cost of one forward pass [27, p. 18], for a window of
1024 tokens per request, and the client checks each proof on a CPU [27, pp. 16–17]. Its Lean proofs
cover the protocol; the equivalence of the executable prover and verifier to it is not proved [27, p. 17].
Such proofs detect a substituted computation; they do not prevent an incorrect response from being
served. If the checking party funds such checks, a budget forces a sample, and the questions become
which jobs to check and how to sanction a provider whose check fails.
    We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identities are
unavailable. The mechanism class excludes entry fees, identity certification, and collateral before

                                                      1
receipts, and a sanctioned provider may re-enter at once under a fresh identity. The rules refund the
escrow of a failed job and exclude the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. The population
is a fixed set of providers, each with one active identity at a time; concurrent aliases, pre-aged
reserve identities, and additional entrants (Sybil attacks [10]) are outside the model.

Checking newcomers is an old idea. Intensive verification until an identity has earned credi-
bility is established: credibility-based spot-checking analyses rejoining under new identities [28, 29];
BOINC offers an optional adaptive-replication policy that replicates a host’s jobs until the host
passes a trust threshold of consecutive valid results [2, 1]; customs guidance recommends checking
all declarations of new importers for a period [35]. Checking can also depend on what a participant
reports [40]; our admissible rules may read offers and claims, but we study targeting by identity age
and verified history. The question we ask is narrower: under a hard audit budget, does R3’s per-
sistent age-ranked targeting do better or worse than targeting by verified history, when everything
else is held equal?

The rule. For B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, immediate free re-
placement restores the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states up to identity relabelling,
so a ban adds no continuation cost; refunds of failed jobs still apply (Corollary 1). R3 gives au-
dit priority to the jobs of identities in their first L rounds, auditing all of them when the round’s
quota covers them, and spends half of the remaining audits on the newest established identities. Its
priority window expires, but the newest-first targeting does not: while a replacement is uniquely
the newest established identity, it receives at least min(s, ⌈r/2⌉) of the r residual audits, s being
its served jobs (Lemma 2 and the remark after it). The lemma bounds the audits a replacement
receives, not its deterrence, and the bound can be zero (ties, no served jobs, newer entrants, or a
quota already spent). Age counts the rounds since an identity’s first offer.

Two registered tests. Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped
before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments.
The first test (October 2026) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results
then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second
test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their
seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition,
stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test’s comparison.

Contributions. Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classi-
fied R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker
gain within the pre-set materiality margin of ±0.05; the second Q1 family mostly repeats the first,
and the classification is established for these six markets, not for the neighbourhood. (ii) In the
measured factorial contrasts, R3’s priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each
other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker’s gain, so given newest-first
targeting the priority phase is close to redundant. (iii) In a registered diagnostic, R3 keeps auditing
an honest provider’s replacement identity well after its window, at a small cost to that provider:
the targeting that works against the attacker also burdens honest newcomers. Supporting these
are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure bound-
aries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and
a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market
replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.


                                                   2
Results in brief. Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets, R3 and C∗ are “equivalent, direction R3
lower” within the registered margin under X7-SC, and likewise under D2+H3: R3 is not materially
better than the development-selected verified-history comparator at the same budget, and the dif-
ference varies in sign across markets. Q2: in the pooled factorial contrasts, the priority phase and
newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects −0.083 and −0.095), and either
one alone accounts for most of the reduction. Q3: the training-selected attack envelopes have lower
measured gains against R3 than against B3 on every evaluated market, a finite-search comparison
that does not establish robustness to the searched families; the selected unconstrained welfare attack
lowers R3’s gross efficiency to 0.840, and exploratory lotteries over existing attacks, selected on the
evaluation records, reach efficiency well below the 0.997 of the registered W1 search at non-negative
estimated gain, with public-family attacks alone; the W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency
bound for randomised policies, and the mixtures’ population profitability is not established. Q4:
the B3–R3 comparison passes on 3 of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; on
the other three, R3’s estimated advantage is positive but below the registered threshold.


2    Related work
Verification-based probation. Credibility-based spot-checking accepts results according to a
worker’s verified history and analyses rejoining under new identities [28, 29]; follow-on work treats
imperfect checks [36]. BOINC’s adaptive replication keeps a consecutive-valid count per host and
trusts a host above a threshold [2, 1]. CrowdEval screens workers with gold tasks before balancing
gold and peer evaluation [26]. Customs guidance recommends checking all declarations of new
importers for a period [35], and a field experiment found no overall compliance gain from close
supervision of new firms [14]. In a model with adverse selection and moral hazard, optimal long-
term contracts screen and then put high types on probation with monitoring that declines with the
track record [5]. Our comparator family adapts the credibility and BOINC rules to a fixed audit
quota (Section 4).

Cheap pseudonyms. With cheap pseudonyms, unfavourable treatment of newcomers is the price
of cooperation [13]; whitewashing degrades peer-to-peer systems when turnover is high [11]; without
a central authority one attacker can create many identities [10]. Reputation mechanisms with pure
moral hazard and the cost of changing identities are analysed in [9]. Reputation without persistent
identity in markets of AI agents, with a one-time cash-out deviation, is studied in [15]; that model
has no verification budget.

Costly verification and audits. Costly state verification goes back to [31]; history-dependent
audit groups under restricted penalties go back to [18]; allocation with costly verification [3], lim-
ited punishments [20], financially constrained agents [21], and ex post verification with limited
penalties [24] assume persistent agents. Recursive inspection games spend a finite stock of in-
spections [34]; audit games optimise randomised inspection against a best-responding attacker [6];
targeted and untargeted inspection under fixed capacity [22] and dynamic monitoring under resource
constraints [30] are also studied; optimal committed audit policies against the worst equilibrium of
the audited agents, with budgeted variants, are derived in [8]. Tournament-based dynamic targeting
allocates a fixed inspection capacity by relative compliance rank and compares it with standards-
based and random inspection [16]; R3 ranks by identity age instead. Our rules are searched and
tuned heuristics, not optima of these models. Paid computation with verification by recomputa-
tion and colluding workers is analysed in [12], and cheating in outsourced distributed computation


                                                  3
in [17].

Verifiable computation. Proofs that a computation was executed as committed now reach large
language models. FlashProve proves, after the fact, that a committed integer version of a 400-billion-
parameter model produced a token as its greedy choice; its Lean proofs cover the protocol, while
the executable prover and verifier were generated by AI and are not proved equivalent to it, and its
technical report states that it was AI-generated and not reviewed in detail [27]. Such proofs check
that a committed computation ran; they do not check the quality of an output, which is what our
audits observe.

Evaluation method. Simulation-based strategic analysis has an established method [37, 38],
including bootstrap inference for empirical games [39]. Weak or incomplete attacks overstate ro-
bustness, and adaptive evaluations must be tailored to each defence [7, 32]. Preregistration separates
generating a hypothesis from testing it on new observations [25]. Our staged registration applies
these tools.


3    The market model
Market. Time is discrete, t = 1, . . . , T , with T = 200 in both tests except the first test’s language-
model attacker (T = 100). In each round J = 50 unit jobs arrive; job j has value vj per unit of
delivered quality, drawn independently and uniformly on [vmin , vmax ]. There are N = 10 providers;
provider i has a quality ceiling qimax , a per-job cost ai + bi q at effort q, a capacity of ki jobs per
round, and an outside option of zero. The provider population and the market parameters of each
test are stated in Sections 7 and 8.

Offers, effort, delivery. In each round an active provider privately chooses an effort qi ∈ [0, qimax ]
for all its jobs and submits a public offer: a claim ci ∈ [0, 1] (a promise that expected delivered
quality is at least ci ) and a per-unit bid. A served job delivers y = qi + ε with ε ∼ N (0, σ 2 ),
independently across jobs; the buyer’s value is vj y. Delivering below the claim is shading.

Rounds and audits. The rule allocates jobs and computes payments, which buyers fund into
escrow. After delivery the rule selects jobs to audit; an audit reveals y exactly at cost α. A job fails
if y < ci − τ , with τ the rule’s tolerance. The rule imposes consequences, and escrow is settled.

Audit budget. Let Ŝ be the expected honest first-best surplus over T rounds and m = Ŝ/nFB
the mean surplus per first-best served job. The operator’s audit budget is B = β Ŝ and the audit
cost α = ρm, so the allowance is B/α = (β/ρ) nFB audits; unspent budget carries over.

Identities. Each provider acts through a current identity. In any round it may retire the identity
and re-enter under a fresh one at entry cost e ≥ 0 (e = 0 in both tests). Rules see identities only
as opaque tokens, must be invariant to relabelling them, and cannot link a fresh identity to its
provider. An identity’s age is the number of rounds since its first offer. Every identity is new at the
start. The honest opponents never replace their identities, so after the start a new identity belongs
to a provider that has replaced, usually the focal one; a truthful focal provider may also replace after
a false failure, so a new identity marks a replacement history, not dishonest delivery. Population
churn among honest opponents is untested, except for the single replacement of the second test’s
honest-replacement diagnostic (Section 10).

                                                   4
Admissible rules. A rule reads only the current offers and job values, the audit results it bought,
its own memory, and the configuration. Payments are funded by buyer escrow. The only conse-
quences are a refund of up to the escrow of an audited job that failed; a ban of the identity for at
most 50 rounds; holding back up to three rounds of an identity’s receipts as a bond; and allocation
priority and audit selection as functions of the allowed inputs. There are no entry fees, no collateral
before receipts, no identity certification, and no consequences for unaudited jobs.

Benchmark and metrics. One focal provider deviates while the others play honestly at full effort
with truthful claims; this is a unilateral deviation, not an equilibrium. For a seed with first-best
surplus S ∗ , the focal provider’s gain is its profit under the attack minus its profit under a selected
truthful reference, divided by S ∗ /N . Gross efficiency is (W + audit cost)/S ∗ , with W the realised
social surplus net of audit cost; net efficiency is W/S ∗ . Gain is a difference of two searched values,
so search error can bias it either way. It is excess profit when replacement is available; it does not
isolate how much of that profit comes from the ability to replace an identity, which would need a
matched comparison with replacement disabled. The second test adds two objectives at its headline
market: welfare attacks that minimise gross efficiency, without a constraint (W0) or subject to a
nonnegative mean normalised gain over the attacker’s reference on the training seeds (W1), whose
profitability is then called supported only if its nominal 95% lower bound on the evaluation seeds
is nonnegative; and coalitions of two or five providers, scored by the total gain Gtot and by the
worst member’s mean gain Gmin , both against a coordinated truthful reference. Coalition results
say nothing about coalition stability.


4    The audit rules we compare
Baselines. B2 posts prices for three claim tiers, allocates by those prices, and audits uniformly.
B3 allocates each job, in descending order of value, to the offer with remaining capacity and the
highest nonnegative score vj ci − bidi , pays vj ci − s2,j with s2,j the highest score among the other
eligible offers (zero if there is none), and audits uniformly at random among served jobs, spread
over the rounds. Both refund a failed job and ban its identity for L rounds.

R3. R3 keeps B3’s allocation, payment, tolerance, and enforcement and changes only which jobs
it audits and when. Pacing: with the integer allowance A = ⌊B/α⌋, R3 reserves min(JL, A) audits
for the last L rounds, in which it audits every served job while audits remain, and spends the rest
evenly over the earlier rounds, carrying unspent quota forward. Selection: it first audits the served
jobs of identities younger than L rounds, newest first; it then spends ⌈r/2⌉ of the r remaining audits
on the newest established identities and the rest uniformly at random.

The factorial (second test). The eight factorial rules and the comparators share B3’s allocation
and payment, refund-and-ban enforcement, no bond, the allowance A = ⌊B/α⌋, and the fixed pair
τ = 0.16, L = 10, which the registered tuning of B3 and of R3 had each chosen. They differ in three
parts: a priority phase P (served jobs of identities younger than L first, newest first), newest-first
residual targeting G (⌈r/2⌉ residual audits to the newest identities), and pacing S (R3’s terminal
reserve and even early quota, against B3’s spread schedule). The rules FP GS include F000 = B3 and
F111 = R3. B2 keeps its posted prices and serves only as a reference at the headline market.

Verified-history targeting (second test). The comparator family adapts credibility-based
spot-checking and BOINC’s adaptive replication to a fixed audit quota. An identity’s credibil-

                                                   5
ity pi is its number of passed audits since its creation or its last failure. It is updated in a batch
after each round’s audits: if any audited job of the identity failed in that round, pi = 0 for the next
round, whatever its passes in the batch; otherwise pi grows by the round’s passes. A replacement
starts at zero, and audits selected in the current round are not passes. C-thr(K) treats an identity
as unproven while pi < K and audits unproven identities first, in ascending pi ; it then selects ⌈r/2⌉
residual jobs by ascending pi plus the picks already assigned to that identity in the round, and the
rest uniformly. C-mem(K, M ) counts only the passes of the last M rounds, so scrutiny returns
to identities that are not audited. C-taper(K) and C-boinc have no priority phase: they audit by
weighted sampling without replacement, with weight K/(pi + K) (C-taper) or 1 if pi < 10 and
1/pi otherwise (C-boinc, after BOINC’s trust threshold of 10 [1]); the weights are relative selection
weights, not audit probabilities. Twelve configurations were compared in development at the fixed
pair and R3’s pacing, by the development tuning objective: the minimum, over the headline market,
a development market, and the attack families, of the smaller of a gross-efficiency margin and an
attacker-gain margin. The efficiency margin was the smaller at every configuration, so the selection
was in effect on gross efficiency, and at the inner search budget every configuration’s selected attack
had zero or negative gain (Table 24). The two best were rechecked at full budget; the selected com-
parator is C∗ = C-mem(K = 6, M = 20), and the runner-up is C-thr(K = 12). Both full-budget
scores were decided by D2 at dev0 (0.103 against 0.098), while under D2+H3 the runner-up left
the lower gain (0.076 against 0.097 at the headline market, 0.018 against 0.038 at dev0). C∗ is
the best of four tested adaptations under the development objective; it is not the strongest possible
credibility rule, and R3 embodies earlier development choices that the comparison does not equalise.
C∗ /S0 is C∗ with B3’s spread pacing.

The repair R4 and tuned variants. R4 is R3 with the terminal reserve capped at
min(JL, ⌊A/2⌋), and C∗ /S2 is C∗ with that schedule. They differ from R3 and C∗ only when
JL > ⌊A/2⌋: they equal them in the second test’s blocks at ρ = 0.2 and appear only in its repair
block at ρ = 1, where R3’s reserve consumes the whole allowance. Separately tuned versions of B3,
R3, and C∗ (B3-t, R3-t, C∗ -t), each over a common grid of 16 tolerance and ban-length pairs and
so possibly at other pairs than the fixed one, are a secondary comparison.


5    What the theory shows
We fix a provider facing honest opponents and an offer with claim 0 < c ≤ q max , and compare
truthful effort c with effort c − ∆, 0 ≤ ∆ ≤ c, at the unchanged offer. We condition on the pre-
delivery state, rival outcomes, and the audit set, all independent of the focal delivery noise, and
assume that focal audit outcomes affect continuation value only through whether any focal job fails,
with the same conditional values under both effort levels. Shading saves g = b∆ per served job
and raises the per-audit failure probability from d0 = Φ(−τ /σ) to d = Φ((∆ − τ )/σ). Let s be the
provider’s served jobs in the round, U the audited subset with K = |U | and escrows Mj , and CU
the difference between the best continuation values after no focal failure and after at least one.

Lemma 1 (local identity). Under these assumptions, the expected profit of shading by ∆ relative to
truthful effort is
                                h                                           i
                   A(∆) = sg − E (d − d0 ) j∈U Mj + (1 − d0 )K − (1 − d)K CU .
                                          P


Corollary 1 (uniform auditing under replacement). If, after an immediate replacement at e = 0, the
distribution of all payoff-relevant future states equals that after no failure up to identity relabelling,

                                                    6
and every continuation available after a failure is also available after no failure up to relabelling (both
hold for B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play
does not depend on the focal identity, when re-entry
                                              P         comes before the next allocation), then CU = 0,
and shading pays whenever sg > (d − d0 ) E j∈U Mj , whatever the ban length.
Lemma 2 (coverage). R3 audits every served job of identities younger than L rounds (age counted
from the first offer) whenever the round’s audit count covers them. Under overload, jobs are audited
by age group, newest first, with k the round’s available audit count; an identity that offers but wins
no jobs still ages; within the focal identity’s group of n jobs, of which s are its own, the number
audited is hypergeometric with k ′ = min{n, max(0, k − H)} draws, H being the fresh jobs of strictly
newer identities.
    After its fresh window, a replacement identity is newer than every initial identity; while it is
uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least min(s, ⌈r/2⌉) of the r residual audits.
In a fixed population in which honest providers do not replace, a provider that keeps replacing can
stay the newest identity, so R3’s targeting of replacements persists beyond the window; with honest
churn it would share the newest positions with honest newcomers.
Theorem 1 (conditional renewal bound). Consider immediate-replacement plans whose play decom-
poses into independent, identically distributed cycles of an established spell followed by a recovery.
Conditional on survival, each established round has expected profit rE and failure probability qE > 0;
the recovery has duration D ≥ L with ED < ∞, profit RF , and NR replacements, with integrable
rewards and charges. The long-run profit per round is Π̄ = (rE /qE + ERF − e ENR )/(1/qE + ED).
If, in addition, truthful play earns a common rate h in both states and, on every recovery history,
every permitted fresh action earns a conditional expected surplus of at most ϵ ≥ 0 over h, then with
e=0
