                                                     1 + qE L
    The factor 1/(1+qE L) is a bound for regenerative policies that satisfy the theorem’s assumptions;
we do not establish those assumptions for the implemented, finite-horizon R3 process, and the
theorem covers neither the adaptive attacker families nor coalitions. No reported result depends on
it.
Proposition 1 (terminal shading). In a round with zero continuation loss, a served provider starting
from a positive truthful effort gains from small same-offer shading whenever b > vmax ϕ(τ /σ)/σ.
    At the first test’s parameters the right-hand side is below 0.067 and every provider has b ≥ 0.22,
so no studied rule eliminates gain; all claims are bounded-gain claims. Proofs are in Appendix B;
the theory checks of the first test are in the supplement.


6    The attacks we test against
Every unilateral attacker family reads only the provider’s own history across its identities, the round,
its own parameters, and the rule’s public constants T and L; it does not observe the remaining global
budget or other identities’ ranks. Coalition members may share their members’ own histories. For
each parametrised family a registered search (CMA-ES with restarts on training seeds) finds the
attack. A rule’s truthful reference is then selected on training seeds from one menu built from
its D2+H3 and X7-SC searches and frozen; the W1 welfare searches, which need that reference,
run after the freeze; coalitions use their own staged joint truthful reference; and no evaluation-seed
episode of a market runs before all of its choices are frozen.

