from the records and reproduces each Q1 category and direction rejection and each Q4 decision.
     Q4 replicates the B3–R3 comparison and R3’s efficiency condition, not the complete amended
claim of the first test. The two questions have separate multiplicity families; no paper-wide five
percent guarantee is claimed. “Confirmatory” means specified before the draw; coverage admission
rests on development fixtures and does not establish nominal coverage on the fresh, selected-rule
distributions. Q4 is an internal replication, not an independent team’s.

Deviations from the protocol. Two arose during the evaluation. First, the runner recalibrates
each fresh market where it runs, and the evaluation ran on Linux x86 machines, while the registered
instance file holds the calibration computed on the author’s arm64 machine. In each fresh market,
between 1 and 6 provider parameters differ between the two in the last binary digit (one unit in
the last place, a relative difference near 10−16 ); the headline market is unaffected. The runner’s


                                                    11
verification compares configuration hashes exactly and refuses these records. We kept them: a
container on the evaluation platform reproduces each run’s configuration hash, every differing value
is within one unit in the last place of the registered one, and verification passes on every other check
when given the reproduced configuration. The check is a script in the artifact. It establishes that the
configurations match, not that the decisions are unchanged, since small input differences can change
discrete simulation branches; the re-evaluation below holds the selected attacks and references fixed
and does not repeat search or reference selection under the registered configurations. We therefore
re-evaluated the frozen policies of every fresh-market cell that Q1 and Q4 use on the registration
platform with the registered configurations, against the frozen references: per-seed gains change in
every cell, cell means move by less than one hundredth, and every Q1 and Q4 decision is unchanged
(docs/revision/deviation/stability.py; Table 19). The report’s addendum lists the hashes,
states the interval labels, and traces search-flag dependencies through each cell’s references, which
qualifies three further decisions as about the prescribed search procedure. Second, the fresh-market
runs cost about ten percent more than estimated, and the author raised the evaluation cap from 40
to 43 USD and the written authorisation from 52 to 55 USD so that every registered cell could run;
no analysis changed.


9    The second test: results
The registered estimates and decisions below come from the report that the tagged code wrote;
the addendum beside it (Section 8) adds labels and disclosures. Table 6 reports the registered
descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic. Tables 4, 5, 9, 13, and 14 report exploratory analyses of
existing records, and Tables 19 and 20 the separate registered-configuration stability check; none
of these changes a registered estimate or decision. Gains are normalised attacker gains; intervals
are those of Table 3. Some descriptive fixed-reference contrasts on fresh1 (12 interval entries in the
report’s per-market Q2 rows) have numerically extreme studentized intervals: the reference-profit
contrast there is above rounding scale on only 3 of 200 seeds (the others are at most 1.2e-13 in
absolute value), and 506 of the 9,999 frozen resamples (5.1 percent) omit all three, so their standard
error is of rounding size and their bootstrap-t statistics are huge. We retain these frozen outputs,
list them in the audit file, and draw no inferential conclusion from them; no other interval in the
report has a bound beyond 1000 in absolute value.

Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1). Pooled over the six fresh markets, the
prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C∗ higher than
against R3 by 0.021 under D2+H3 and by 0.024 under X7-SC; the mean gains are 0.086 against C∗
and 0.065 against R3 under D2+H3, and 0.091 and 0.067 under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject
d = 0 (calibrated input p′ = 0.0004; Holm-adjusted p = 0.0008 for each). Both intervals lie inside
the registered margin of ±0.05: the categories are “equivalent, direction R3 lower” (D2+H3) and
“equivalent, direction R3 lower” (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is “efficiency condition
met”. Under the registered margin, R3 is not materially better than the development-selected
comparator; the margin of ±0.05 is half the first test’s comparative margin, not an estimated
threshold of buyer indifference, and a narrower margin could order the two rules. The difference
changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from −0.038 on fresh3 to 0.053
on fresh1 under D2+H3. Search uncertainty matters at this level: evaluating the doubled-search
winners on the same seeds against the same frozen references (Table 5, exploratory) changes fresh3’s
difference from −0.038 to 0.015, and the pooled differences to 0.030 under D2+H3 and 0.024 under
X7-SC; no decision is recomputed. The registered intervals measure seed uncertainty in six fixed


