not establish the category for the neighbourhood; the X7-SC interval excludes zero and lies inside
the margin, but that family mostly repeats D2+H3 (below). In relative terms, under D2+H3, the
attacker’s pooled mean gain against C∗ exceeds that against R3 by a fraction of 0.319. The two
Q1 families are close to one test: X7-SC’s search starts from the embedded D2+H3 winner, and
in 10 of the 12 Q1 cells it selected that winner, with identical evaluated gains; only fresh3 against
C∗ and fresh4 against R3 differ. With the headline market, the same holds in 12 of 14 market–rule
cells, which is why most of prediction Q3-a’s differences are exactly zero. With each rule’s strongest
training-selected family instead (Table 15), the pooled difference is 0.031, and 0.070 on fresh1, where
X5-AG/v2 is C∗ ’s strongest family. C∗ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect,
and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain than C∗ (Section 4); a comparator
selected on attacker gain could change the direction label. The two rules differ more in whom they
burden than in the attacker’s gain. In the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic at
the headline market (Table 6), an honest provider replaces its identity at round 50: R3 audits 1.000
and 0.917 of the replacement’s served jobs in its first two ten-round age bins, C∗ audits 0.428 and
0.161, and B3 about 0.250. Under R3 the replacement earns slightly less per round and loses more
receipts to refunds paid to buyers on false audit failures; R3 directs more of the fixed audit capacity
to this provider, which does not show higher total audit spending. Both decisions are about the
prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses
them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the
fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section 8). The paired descriptive differences in Table 8 are small
for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction; the
reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting d with the same normalisation into the
attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table 4, exploratory) shows that almost all of it
is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C∗ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.

