Which parts of R3 matter (Q2). Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the
fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are −0.083 for the priority phase P and −0.095 for
newest-first residual targeting G, both reductions, and 0.004 for pacing S, a small increase (X7-
SC: 0.005). The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference
between G’s conditional effects, is 0.082; G changes the gain by −0.177 without the priority phase
and by only −0.014 with it, and the priority phase changes it by only −0.001 once G is in place,
so given newest-first targeting the priority phase is close to redundant in this model. In the pooled
contrasts, either P or G alone accounts for most of the measured reduction; adding the other
contributes little. The pattern also appears in the fixed-attack contrasts averaged over the eight
selected attacks (P: −0.087). Our registered conjectures (P negligible, the targeting parts additive,
S helpful) failed as scored (Table 22); the stricter eligibility reading of Table 21 leaves them not
assessed, with the estimates unchanged. X7-SC agrees (Table 12).

