core rule because of search flags, and so is R3’s robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry
no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the
selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency 0.839 for B3 and 0.840 for R3, with
gains relative to the truthful reference of 0.254 and −0.094; C∗ is like R3. Under the prescribed
W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency 0.997 for R3,
while B3 stays at 0.839 (Table 16). R3’s selected W1 policy has an estimated gain of exactly zero,
and the doubled W1 searches for R3 and C∗ select truthful delivery (zero shading), with the option
to replace after a failed audit left on: the W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against
them. These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class.
An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each
episode, has the probability-weighted mean of its components’ gains and efficiencies, so it satisfies
a mean-gain constraint only when those weighted gains do; the following rows are that arithmetic
on the existing evaluation records. With the H4 attack as companion, at W0 weights selected on
the evaluation records, the mixtures reach gross efficiencies of 0.932 for R3 and 0.921 for C∗ at non-
negative estimated gain; with public D2+H3 companions, R3’s mixture (0.945) meets the registered
training constraint, and C∗ ’s does so at the smaller weight 47.65 percent, with efficiency 0.9224
against 0.997 under the registered W1 search (Tables 13 and 14). Weights and companions were
chosen on the evaluation seeds with an unadjusted nominal one-sided 95 percent bound, so these
rows show that the W1 policy class is not closed under randomisation; they are not out-of-sample
estimates, and population profitability remains unestablished. The mixture efficiencies stay above
0.90; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised
members, the selected total gains are 0.584 against B3 and 0.058 against R3 (search flagged); for
five, 0.191 and 0.108 (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective
leaves R3’s worst member a mean gain of −0.001; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives 0.024
against B3 and 0.025 against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an
ordering across every coalition family (Table 17). At ρ = 1, where R3’s terminal reserve collapses,
R3 leaves 0.565 under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to 0.356 and C∗ with the capped schedule
(C∗ /S2) to 0.434 (Table 18).

