a flagged search is itself incomplete. Table 21 adds two post hoc eligibility readings. Starting
from the listed inputs of each scored comparison, we propagate flags through recorded upstream
links, including references, attack banks, and warm starts. An inherited flag identifies a potentially
relevant ancestor; it does not establish that the flagged candidate was selected or that the statistic
would change. The map is released with the scorer, and neither sensitivity is designated as the official
retrospective score. If a flagged listed input or recorded ancestor makes a Q3 policy comparison
ineligible, Q3-a, Q3-b, Q3-d, and Q3-e are not assessed: 6 predictions are fulfilled, 8 fail, and 4 are
not assessed (counting direct inputs only, Q3-e stays fulfilled: 7, 8, and 3). The broader sensitivity
excludes flagged scored cells outside six decision-related predictions retained by convention: Q1-a,
its alternative, Q1-b, Q1-d, Q4-a, and Q4-b (the design keeps the decisions but does not say how
predictions about them are scored). A prediction still fails if any eligible cell fails; otherwise, an
excluded required cell leaves it not assessed. Q1-c and Q2-a to Q2-d are then also not assessed: 5
fulfilled, 4 failed, and 9 not assessed (counting direct inputs only: 6, 5, and 7). No reading turns a
failed prediction into a fulfilled one or changes a registered decision; the marked rows of Tables 22
and 23 are those a stricter reading changes, and their estimates stand as estimates of the prescribed
searches. The predictions differ in stakes and are not independent (Q1-a, its alternative, and Q1-d
are decided by the same Q1 categories, and Q3-e passes by less than one hundred-thousandth), so
the count of fulfilled predictions is not a validation score. The failures are informative: we expected
the priority phase to matter little, the replication to hold on at least five markets, X5-AG/v2 to
stay below D2+H3, and the unconstrained welfare attack to leave R3’s and C∗ ’s efficiency at the
registered level, and none of these held (the first is not assessed under the broader reading).



